---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Claim from '../components/content/Claim.astro';
import ExtLink from '../components/content/ExtLink.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Colophon · Utkarsh Amaresh"
  description="How this site is built: a static Astro site with typed content, self-hosted fonts, and accessibility, performance and evidence checks on every build."
>
  <article class="container colophon">
    <h1>Colophon</h1>
    <p class="lead">How this site is built, and the rules it holds itself to.</p>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="architecture">Static first</h2>
      <p>
        The site is built with <ExtLink href="https://astro.build/">Astro</ExtLink> as plain static HTML.
        There is no client framework: the only JavaScript is a few small scripts for the theme toggle, the
        copy-email button and the case-study contents, and every page works without them.
      </p>

      <h2 id="content">Typed content</h2>
      <p>
        Pages are written in MDX and TypeScript in strict mode. Every number and strong claim is rendered from
        one registry that records its source, and links to that source. The build checks the registry before
        it publishes anything: a page that names a claim the registry doesn't have, or a claim without a
        public source, stops the build.
      </p>

      <h2 id="type">Type and visuals</h2>
      <p>
        Set in IBM Plex Sans and IBM Plex Mono, self-hosted (no third-party font requests). Colours are a
        small set of design tokens with light and dark themes; diagrams are hand-drawn SVG with a text version
        for screen readers. Photos are real and cropped so the subject is me.
      </p>

      <h2 id="testing">Accessibility and testing</h2>
      <p>
        Every page is tested in a real browser at phone, tablet and desktop widths, in light and dark themes
        and with reduced motion: automated accessibility checks, keyboard navigation, visible focus, heading
        structure, and no horizontal scrolling. Lighthouse runs against the built site, and a separate scan of
        the output makes sure nothing private, such as secrets, local file paths or unpublished project names,
        ends up in what's deployed.
      </p>

      <h2 id="performance">Performance</h2>
      <p>
        Each page type has a byte budget for HTML, CSS, JavaScript, fonts and images, and the build fails if a
        page goes over it. No analytics, trackers or third-party scripts are loaded.
      </p>

      <h2 id="ai">On using AI tools</h2>
      <p>
        <Claim id="about.ai-statement" marker={false}>
          I use AI coding assistants as part of my everyday workflow: for drafting code, tests and
          documentation, and for exploring alternatives quickly. I remain responsible for everything that
          ships. I set the architecture and record design decisions, review each change, verify behaviour with
          tests and CI rather than trusting output, and own the debugging, security choices, deployment and
          final call on each decision.
        </Claim>
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .colophon {
    padding-top: var(--space-8);
  }
  .lead {
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
    font-size: var(--text-md);
    line-height: var(--lh-md);
    color: var(--ink-2);
    max-width: var(--measure-lead);
  }
  .prose {
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
  }
  .prose h2:first-child {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
</style>
